Here is the study in JSON format with logical operators:

```
{
  "AND": [
    {
      "AND": [
        {
          "raw_text": "Aged at least 18 years"
        },
        {
          "raw_text": "with an ability and willingness to give written informed consent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "AND": [
        {
          "raw_text": "Body mass index"
        },
        {
          "OR": [
            {
              "raw_text": "25 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "26 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "27 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "28 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "29 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "30 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "31 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "32 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "33 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "34 kg/m2"
            },
            {
              "raw_text": "35 kg/m2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "AND": [
        {
          "raw_text": "Users of at least"
        },
        {
          "OR": [
            {
              "raw_text": "2 cups"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "raw_text": "of caffeinated coffee per day"
        },
        {
          "raw_text": "who are willing to be randomized to any of the interventions"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "raw_text": "Non-smoking"
    }
  ]
}
```

Note that I assumed the BMI range is 25-35 kg/m2, and the caffeinated coffee consumption is at least 2 cups per day. Also, I used the `OR` operator to include the range of BMI values, and the `AND` operator to combine the conditions.